%S A401246 1,1,2,1,3,1,1,2,4,1,1,3,1,5,1,1,3,1,6,1,2,1,3,1,7,1,3,1,1,2,4,1,8,1,
%T A401246 3,1,1,3,1,5,1,9,1,3,1,1,3,1,6,1,2,10,1,3,1,1,3,1,7,1,3,1,11,1,3,1,1,
%U A401246 3,1,7,1,3,1,12,1,2,4,1,1,3,1,7,1,3,1,13,1,3,1,5,1
%N A401246 Number T(n,k) of nodes in the subtree whose root is located at position k in the inorder traversal of a complete binary tree (heap structure) on n elements; triangle T(n,k), n>=1, 1<=k<=n, read by rows.
%C A401246 If the terms of row n in (weakly) decreasing order are multiplied with [1,2,3,...], the sum of the products is A122247(n). For row n=5 we have [5,3,1,1,1] * [1,2,3,4,5] = [5,6,3,4,5] with sum 23 = A122247(5).
%H A401246 Alois P. Heinz, <a href="/A401246/b401246.txt">Rows n = 1..255, flattened</a>
%H A401246 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/Heap.html">Heap</a>
%H A401246 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Binary_heap">Binary heap</a>
%H A401246 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tree_traversal">Tree traversal</a>
%F A401246 Product_{k=1..n} T(n,k) = A132862(n) for n>=0.
%F A401246 n! / Product_{k=1..n} T(n,k) = A056971(n) for n>=0.
%F A401246 1/n * Product_{k=1..n} T(n,k) = A133385(n-1) for n>=1.
%F A401246 T(n,A078881(n)) = n.
%F A401246 T(2^n-1,2^(n-1)) = 2^n - 1 = A000225(n).
%F A401246 T(n,k) = T(n,n+1-k) if n = 2^i - 1 with i>=1.
%e A401246 For a heap with 5 elements, the labels (k,N) of the nodes in this tree indicate the position k in an inorder traversal, as well as the number of nodes N in the subtree:
%e A401246           (4,5)
%e A401246          /     \
%e A401246      (2,3)     (5,1)
%e A401246      /   \
%e A401246   (1,1) (3,1)
%e A401246 So row n=5 is: [1, 3, 1, 5, 1]. The product is 1*3*1*5*1 = 15 = A132862(5). And the number of (binary) heaps on 5 elements is 5!/15 = 120/15 = 8 = A056971(5).
%e A401246 Triangle T(n,k) begins:
%e A401246   1;
%e A401246   1, 2;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1;
%e A401246   1, 2, 4, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 5, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 6, 1, 2;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1;
%e A401246   1, 2, 4, 1, 8, 1,  3,  1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 5, 1, 9,  1,  3, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 6, 1, 2, 10,  1, 3, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1, 11, 1, 3, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1, 12, 1, 2, 4, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1, 13, 1, 3, 1, 5, 1;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1, 14, 1, 3, 1, 6, 1, 2;
%e A401246   1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3,  1, 15, 1, 3, 1, 7, 1, 3, 1;
%e A401246   ...
%p A401246 g:= proc(n) option remember; `if`(n=0, [], (b-> (f->
%p A401246       [g(f)[], n, g(n-1-f)[]])(min(b-1, n-b/2)))(2^ilog2(n)))
%p A401246     end:
%p A401246 T:= (n, k)-> g(n)[k]:
%p A401246 seq(seq(T(n, k), k=1..n), n=1..15);
%Y A401246 Row sums give A001855(n+1).
%Y A401246 Row products give A132862 (for n>=0).
%Y A401246 T(2n-1,n) gives A038712.
%Y A401246 Index of n in row n gives A078881(n) = A006165(n+1) = 1+A279521(n).
%Y A401246 The number of 1's in row n gives A110654.
%Y A401246 The number of 2's in row n gives A059841.
%Y A401246 The number of 3's in row n gives A059169.
%Y A401246 The number of distinct terms in row n gives A294991(n+1)-1.
%Y A401246 The number of even terms in row 2n gives A029837(n+1) = A070939(n) = A113473(n).
%Y A401246 The number of odd terms in row 2n gives A049039(n).
%Y A401246 The number of odd terms in row 2n-1 gives A005408(n-1).
%Y A401246 Cf. A000142, A000225, A056971, A122247, A133385.
%K A401246 nonn,look,tabl,new
%O A401246 1,3
